      "style": "Acoustic folk ballad, fingerpicked steel-string guitar and restrained brushed percussion, low warm male vocal with plainspoken intimate delivery, patient verse pulse into a rising singalong chorus, close dry room sound with subtle harmony on final lines\n\nTake 2: Acoustic folk ballad, patient verse pulse, fingerpicked steel-string guitar and restrained brushed percussion, close dry room sound, low warm male vocal with plainspoken intimate delivery, rising singalong chorus and subtle harmony on final lines",
      "lyric": "[Verse 1]\nThe morning train was pulling out\nYou held my coat, then let it go\nI watched your hand become a doubt\nA small white flag beneath the snow\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI said I’d follow, said I’d try\nYou said, “Don’t make this hard”\nThen all the words we used to mean\nLay face-down in the yard\n\n[Chorus]\nGo on, go on, I’ll stay behind\nYou take the road, I’ll take my time\nGo on, go on, don’t turn around\nI’m learning what gets left in town\nOh, I’m learning what gets left in town\n\n[Verse 2]\nYour coffee cup is on the sill\nA ring of brown, a hollow moon\nThe house is quiet, standing still\nLike it already missed you too\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI kept your name inside my teeth\nTill it cut my mouth to say\nSome things don’t break with one hard blow\nThey wear out day by day\n\n[Chorus]\nGo on, go on, I’ll stay behind\nYou take the road, I’ll take my time\nGo on, go on, don’t turn around\nI’m learning what gets left in town\nOh, I’m learning what gets left in town\n\n[Bridge]\n(Ah-ah, ah-ah)\nI won’t call it mercy\nI won’t call it fate\nYou found a door to walk right through\nI found the latch too late\n\nIf being strong means standing still\nWhile everybody flies\nThen let me be the stubborn earth\nBeneath your borrowed sky\n\n[Final Chorus]\nGo on, go on, I’ll stay behind\nYou take the road, I’ll take my time\nGo on, go on, don’t turn around\nI’m more than what gets left in town\nOh, I’m more than what gets left in town\n\n[Outro]\nThe morning train is long gone now\nThe rails still sing below\nI hold my coat around my heart\nAnd take the road I know",
