      "tag": "constant fills",
      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write plainly prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel left behind where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of soul while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get unvarnished with this one.",
      "style": "variety 1/4, raw contemporary soul, slow gospel-leaning groove, warm electric piano, restrained bass, brushed drums, muted guitar fills, close-miked expressive lead vocal, verses held low and conversational, pre-chorus climbing with tension, chorus opening into a sing-along release, call-and-response backing vocals, organic room ambience, dynamic final chorus with tasteful vocal ad-libs, intimate analog mix\n\nTake 2: undefined",
      "lyric": "— TAKE 1 —\n\n[Verse 1]\nYou packed your blue coat by the kitchen chair\nLeft one cup drying, left me standing there\nThe train pulled out at half past five\nYou had a ticket, I had a life\n\nI called your name till my throat went dry\nWatched your red scarf disappear from sight\nThey said, \"Give it time, you'll be alright\"\nBut time don't sleep here every night\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI keep the door on the broken chain\nLike you might turn back through the falling rain\nBut hope is a house with a leaking roof\nAnd I am tired of hiding from the truth\n\n[Chorus]\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nWith your goodbye still changing shape in my mind\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nLearning how to lose you one day at a time\nOh, I tried, I tried, I tried\nBut a heart can't cross what a train leaves behind\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nStill saying your name when I turn out the light\n\n[Verse 2]\nYour mother called on a Sunday noon\nAsked if I heard from you too soon\nI lied and said, \"We're doing fine\"\nThen folded your shirt for the hundredth time\n\nThe neighbors quit asking where you went\nThe rent went up, the winter came and bent\nThe little tree we planted by the gate\nIt grew without you, and I learned to wait\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI keep your number beneath my tongue\nA bitter little prayer from when we were young\nBut love is a door that swings both ways\nAnd yours shut hard on the better days\n\n[Chorus]\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nWith your goodbye still changing shape in my mind\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nLearning how to lose you one day at a time\nOh, I tried, I tried, I tried\nBut a heart can't cross what a train leaves behind\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nStill saying your name when I turn out the light\n\n[Bridge]\n(Oh, let me go)\nI don't need a reason dressed up clean\nI don't need the version that makes you kind\nYou chose the road, you chose the leaving\nI was the one you left to find\n\n(And I am still here)\nStill here, still breathing, still paying the cost\nStill sweeping up the pieces of what we lost\nIf being left is all you gave to me\nI'll make a life from what you couldn't see\n\n[Final Chorus]\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nBut your goodbye ain't the ruler of my life\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nNow I'm taking back this heart you left to dry\nOh, I cried, I cried, I cried\nTill the tears had nowhere left to hide\nYou moved on, I stayed behind\nNow I walk through the door and leave you on the other side\n\n[Outro]\n(Leave you on the other side)\nI stayed behind, but I did not disappear\nI stayed behind, and I am still here\nStill here, still here\nI am still here\n\n— TAKE 2 —\n\n",
