      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write plainly prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel left behind where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of reggae while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get unvarnished with this one.",
      "style": "Roots reggae with warm rounded bass, clipped offbeat guitar, rim clicks, hand percussion and restrained organ bubbles; weathered female lead with gentle harmony responses, intimate verses opening into a communal singalong chorus; spacious analog mix with subtle dub delay on phrase endings; steady one-drop groove.\n\nTake 2: Reggae, roots reggae with a spacious analog mix and subtle dub delay on phrase endings; warm rounded bass, clipped offbeat guitar, rim clicks, hand percussion, restrained organ bubbles; steady one-drop groove; weathered female lead vocal, intimate verses opening into a communal singalong chorus with gentle harmony responses",
      "lyric": "[Verse 1]\nYou said, “Wait here, I’ll be back by dawn,”\nThen the station clock kept carrying on.\nYour blue coat hung by the kitchen door,\nI kept one cup out, though you drank no more.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI learned the road by the sound of your name,\nI learned a heart can wait through shame.\n\n[Chorus]\nLeft behind, left behind,\nI’m still standing where you changed your mind.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nYou took the morning, left me the night.\nOh, I bend, but I don’t break,\nI bless the love I couldn’t make.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nStill got a little fire in this tired life.\n\n[Verse 2]\nThe neighbors ask if I heard from you,\nI say, “Some things fade, some things come true.”\nYour old key sleeps in a coffee tin,\nA small brass proof of the shape I was in.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI wore your promise thin as thread,\nStill heard your footsteps in my head.\n\n[Chorus]\nLeft behind, left behind,\nI’m still standing where you changed your mind.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nYou took the morning, left me the night.\nOh, I bend, but I don’t break,\nI bless the love I couldn’t make.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nStill got a little fire in this tired life.\n\n[Bridge]\n(Oh-oh, carry me, carry me)\nI won’t call that silence home again.\n(Oh-oh, set me free, set me free)\nA closed door is not the end.\nI lost your hand, I found my ground,\nThere’s more than one way out of town.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nLeft behind, left behind,\nI’m not the person you left that night.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nI took my sorrow and made it mine.\nOh, I bend, but I don’t break,\nI bless the love I couldn’t make.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nI’ve got a little fire, and it’s burning bright.\n\n[Outro]\nNo more waiting by the door,\nNo more being less than more.\nLeft behind, left behind,\nI’m moving on in my own time.",
