      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write feverishly prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel stubborn homesickness where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of disco while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select from agriculture but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get wry with this one.",
      "style": "Disco, elastic bass guitar, clipped rhythm guitar, bright string stabs and handclaps, four-on-the-floor groove, playful wry male lead with tight conversational verses, stacked pre-chorus harmonies, instantly singable vowel-rich vocalese chorus and cathartic final chorus, polished dancefloor mix\n\nTake 2: Disco with a playful wry male lead vocal, elastic bass guitar, clipped rhythm guitar, bright string stabs and handclaps, polished dancefloor mix, four-on-the-floor groove; tight conversational verses, stacked-harmony pre-chorus rising into an instantly singable vowel-rich chorus, cathartic final chorus",
      "lyric": "[Verse 1]\nI left when the wheat went gold,\nWith a borrowed coat and a story to sell,\nSaid, “Home is a word you fold\nWhen the road rings a dinner bell.”\nNow the county fair has crowned\nA pumpkin shaped like a mayor’s head,\nAnd every road I’ve found\nStill points to the room I left.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, I made my bed in a different field,\nI learned what a hard-earned harvest yields,\nBut the heart’s a mule with a stubborn gait—\nIt hauls old weather through every gate.\n\n[Chorus]\nI’m coming home, hey-oh, hey-oh,\nThough my boots say no, my blood says go,\nA little too proud, a little too late,\nStill I’m knocking on the garden gate.\nOh, row by row, I reap what I sow,\nBut nobody tells you where the lost things grow,\nSo save me a chair, let the kettle sing—\nI’m homesick for everything.\n\n[Verse 2]\nThe combine coughed at dawn,\nLike an uncle with a joke gone sour,\nWe watched the last load roll on,\nThen argued about the price per hour.\nI laughed till the tears came through—\nVery dignified, very farm-to-table,\nFor a person who swore they knew\nHow to leave whenever they were able.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, I wore my freedom like Sunday shoes,\nShined up bright with a heel to lose,\nBut the heart keeps time with a crooked drum—\nIt knows the way before I come.\n\n[Chorus]\nI’m coming home, hey-oh, hey-oh,\nThough my boots say no, my blood says go,\nA little too proud, a little too late,\nStill I’m knocking on the garden gate.\nOh, row by row, I reap what I sow,\nBut nobody tells you where the lost things grow,\nSo save me a chair, let the kettle sing—\nI’m homesick for everything.\n\n[Bridge]\n(la-da-dee, la-da-doo)\nI miss the screen door’s rude review,\nThe dog that judged my every shoe,\nThe neighbor’s tractor, three towns loud,\nThe rain barrel wearing a silver crown.\nI miss the way the kitchen knew\nExactly what a body needed to do—\nSit down, shut up, eat your fill,\nBe loved against your will.\n\n[Breakdown]\nHey-oh, hey-oh, turn that key,\nThe harvest took the best of me,\nHey-oh, hey-oh, let me in,\nI’ve been a stranger to my own skin.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nI’m coming home, hey-oh, hey-oh,\nLet the whole wide world collect its dough,\nA little more humble, a little less late,\nI’m dancing barefoot at the garden gate.\nOh, row by row, I reap what I sow,\nAnd now I know where the lost things grow:\nIn the back-room laugh, in the kettle’s ring,\nIn the stubborn ache of everything.\n\n[Outro]\n(hey-oh, hey-oh, la-da-dee)\nHome is the crop that keeps finding me,\n(hey-oh, hey-oh, la-da-doo)\nI left for good—then I came through.",
