      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write bitterly prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel quiet tenderness where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of electro while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select from astronomy but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get wry with this one.",
      "style": "Electro art-pop, tight four-on-the-floor pulse, rubbery synth bass, glassy arpeggiator and clipped drum-machine percussion; polished nocturnal mix with controlled width and subtle saturation; dry intimate lead vocal with sly theatrical phrasing, restrained conversational verses, widening pre-chorus harmonies, bright doubles and highly singable stacked vowel hooks\n\nTake 2: electro art-pop; rubbery synth bass, glassy arpeggiator, clipped drum-machine percussion; polished nocturnal mix with dry edges and controlled stereo bloom; tight four-on-the-floor pulse; dry intimate lead vocal with sly theatrical phrasing, restrained conversational verses, widening pre-chorus harmonies, bright doubled chorus and stacked vowel hooks",
      "lyric": "[Verse 1]\nYou left your coffee going cold by the phone,\nA little brown planet with a ring of foam.\nI said, “That’s fine,” like a person with sense—\nThen alphabetized the evidence.\n\nYour key still sulks in the blue ceramic dish,\nA tiny metal moon with a foolish wish.\nI gave it a name, then I gave it the boot;\nNow it keeps orbiting every room.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nAnd I know, I know, I know the drill:\nThings fall away, but they don’t sit still.\n\n[Chorus]\nOh, go on, go dark, my dear,\nMake a clean eclipse of the atmosphere.\nBye-bye, bright side, adieu—\nI’ll be fine in a minute or two.\n(ah-ah, ooh-ooh)\nOh, go on, go dark, my dear,\nLeave your little weather down here.\nBye-bye, bright side, adieu—\nI’ll be fine if I lie like you.\n\n[Verse 2]\nThe news said a satellite lost its course,\nNo tragic music, no visible remorse.\nIt burned through the sky with impeccable style—\nA brief little scandal, a dangerous smile.\n\nI thought of your coat on the back of the chair,\nStill holding the shape of somebody there.\nThe tailor was honest: “It’s only a sleeve.”\nHow rude of the truth to make me believe.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nAnd I know, I know, I know the score:\nYou can’t call it home if it won’t land anymore.\n\n[Chorus]\nOh, go on, go dark, my dear,\nMake a clean eclipse of the atmosphere.\nBye-bye, bright side, adieu—\nI’ll be fine in a minute or two.\n(ah-ah, ooh-ooh)\nOh, go on, go dark, my dear,\nLeave your little weather down here.\nBye-bye, bright side, adieu—\nI’ll be fine if I lie like you.\n\n[Bridge]\nNo hard feelings, no, not me—\nI only checked the door at half past three.\nNo hard feelings, hand on heart—\nI only kept your side of the bed intact.\n\nIf love’s a science, I’m a charming fraud,\nMaking charts for a merciful god.\nThere goes the signal, there goes the spark—\nPlease don’t come back and ruin the dark.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nOh, go on, go dark, my dear,\nMake a clean eclipse of the atmosphere.\nBye-bye, bright side, adieu—\nI’ll be fine in a minute or two.\n(ah-ah, ooh-ooh)\nOh, go on, go dark, my dear,\nI’ll keep your weather down here.\nBye-bye, bright side, adieu—\nIf I sing it, it disappears.\n\n[Outro]\nA little brown planet, a ring of foam,\nStill making a universe out of home.\n(ah-ah, ooh-ooh)\nGo dark, go dark—\nI’ll manage the stars.",
