      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write feverishly prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel misplaced homesickness where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of breakbeat while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select from railways but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get superstitious with this one.",
      "style": "variety 1/4, feverish breakbeat art-pop, restless chopped drums, syncopated bass, tense piano ostinato, metallic percussive accents, dramatic minor-key harmonic swells, intimate verses exploding into cathartic singalong hooks, agile expressive lead vocal, vowel-rich melodic vocalese, tight dry verses opening into wide reverberant choruses, escalating final refrain",
      "lyric": "[Intro]\n(keep count, keep count)\nAy-eh, oh-oh, ah-ah\nOne for the whistle, two for the rain\nThree for the name I refuse to say\n\n[Verse 1]\nA blackbird landed on platform nine,\nThree pecks hard on the timetable line.\nThe porter crossed himself, then looked away—\nSaid, “Some trains leave twice, but not today.”\n\nI had your address in the lining of my coat,\nA little red thread and a borrowed note.\nThe rails went silver beneath my feet,\nEvery mile back home made the leaving complete.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, I know that road, I know that turn,\nI know which bridges creak and burn.\nBut the house I miss, when I get near,\nMoves one station farther every year.\n\n[Chorus]\nTake me home, take me home—\nBut home won’t hold me when I come.\nI knock three times, I bite my tongue,\nThe old door opens; I’m still no one.\n\nAy-eh, oh-oh, carry me slow,\nBack where the lost lights used to glow.\nTake me home, take me home—\nI’m homesick for a place I’ve outgrown.\n\n[Verse 2]\nAt the junction, the signal flashed red,\nThough the line was clear as the words you said.\nA coin on the track, a fox in the fog,\nA wedding ring nailed to a telegraph log.\n\nI laughed like a fool—what else could I do?\nThe night had a mouth and it sounded like you.\nThen the whistle wailed from the cut in the hill,\nAnd every old promise went suddenly still.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, I know that road, I know that name,\nI know the match that starts the flame.\nBut the room I miss, when I get there,\nHas my shape in it—and nobody cares.\n\n[Chorus]\nTake me home, take me home—\nBut home won’t hold me when I come.\nI knock three times, I bite my tongue,\nThe old door opens; I’m still no one.\n\nAy-eh, oh-oh, carry me slow,\nBack where the lost lights used to glow.\nTake me home, take me home—\nI’m homesick for a place I’ve outgrown.\n\n[Bridge]\n(If you see a fox, don’t follow.)\nIf you hear your name, don’t turn.\nIf the carriage counts you twice,\nPay the fare and let it burn.\n\nI kept one seat for the life we had,\nKept one lie because it hurt less bad.\nNow the train runs clean through the dark,\nAnd the moon leaves ash on every mark.\n\n[Breakdown]\nHome—oh, home—no, not there.\nHome—oh, home—thin cold air.\nHa-ah, hey-eh, I know, I know—\nThe nearer I get, the farther I go.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nTake me home, take me home—\nLet the iron sing, let the dead bells hum.\nI knock three times, I bite my tongue,\nThe old door opens; I’m still no one.\n\nAy-eh, oh-oh, carry me slow,\nBack where the lost lights used to glow.\nTake me home, take me home—\nI’m homesick for a place I’ve outgrown.\n\nTake me home, take me home—\nSay my name like you mean someone.\nI knock three times, the dawn comes numb—\nThe old door opens; I’m still no one.\n\n[Outro]\n(keep count, keep count)\nOne for the whistle, two for the rain,\nThree for the home that won’t take my name.\nAy-eh, oh-oh, ah-ah—\nI leave by the line, I return by the scar.",
