      "style": "Jungle with a polished yet raw club mix, sudden dropouts and sharp transient control; chopped Amen-style breakbeat drums, sub-heavy rolling bass, tense minor-key pads and smoky atmospheric textures; intimate breathy male lead with tight syncopated phrasing, vocal chops as emotional punctuation, cathartic anthemic chorus; restless high-energy 174 BPM drive.\n\nTake 2: Jungle, intimate breathy male lead with tight syncopated phrasing and anthemic chorus; 174 BPM restless breakbeat drive; chopped amen-style drums, sub-heavy rolling bass, tense minor-key pads and smoky atmospheric textures; sudden dropouts, emotionally punctuating vocal chops, polished yet raw club mix",
      "lyric": "[Verse 1]\nYou got out clean, I got the soot\nA mouthful of metal, a borrowed boot\nThe bell went blue in the engine bay\nYou found the morning, I found the gray\n\nI don’t begrudge you, that’s the lie\nI wear it thin till the seams go white\nYou kept your name, you kept your skin\nI kept the door that wouldn’t give in\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, I know, I know, I know\nSomebody has to let it go\nBut every time your lungs draw air\nI taste the room that wasn’t there\n\n[Chorus]\nLucky you, lucky you—say it again\nYou got a sky, I got a ceiling of flame\nLucky you, lucky you, don’t look back\nI’m still chewing on the smoke in the black\n(ah-ah, oh-oh)\nIf I could trade this ache for your ordinary day\nI’d bite my tongue clean just to hear you say\nLucky you, lucky you—what a beautiful bruise\nI hate that I need what I envy in you\n\n[Verse 2]\nYou sleep with the window open wide\nI count the exits on either side\nYou call it weather, call it rain\nI hear the roof come down again\n\nThe kettle sings, your hallway glows\nNo one is shouting, nobody knows\nYou set the table, you break the bread\nI set one place for the nearly dead\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, I know, I know, I know\nAsh makes a liar of the snow\nYou say the world is soft and fair\nI smell the hose-line in your hair\n\n[Chorus]\nLucky you, lucky you—say it again\nYou got a sky, I got a ceiling of flame\nLucky you, lucky you, don’t look back\nI’m still chewing on the smoke in the black\n(ah-ah, oh-oh)\nIf I could trade this ache for your ordinary day\nI’d bite my tongue clean just to hear you say\nLucky you, lucky you—what a beautiful bruise\nI hate that I need what I envy in you\n\n[Bridge]\nWhen the alarm quits ringing\nAnd the red doors close\nThere’s a little life singing\nWhere the dead air goes\n\nI want your small forgiveness\nYour cheap and faithful light\nThe kind of fear that leaves you\nBefore you leave the night\n\n(uh-uh, ah-ah)\nDon’t call me healed, call me hungry\nDon’t call me brave, call me late\nI’m learning to hold what the fire couldn’t\nI’m learning to want without turning to hate\n\n[Final Chorus]\nLucky you, lucky you—now I can hear\nThe ordinary world coming back through my fear\nLucky you, lucky you, stay right there\nLet me borrow the warmth of your unburnt air\n(ah-ah, oh-oh)\nI won’t make a home of the room I escaped\nI won’t make a god of the life you were spared\nLucky you, lucky you—let the hard truth bruise\nI can love what I lost without stealing from you\n\n[Outro]\nLucky you—breathe it in\nLucky you—the light gets in\n(ah-ah, oh-oh)\nI was hungry, I was used\nNow I’m here, and so are you",
