      "tag": "slots left to Suno",
      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write [INSERT adverb] prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel [INSERT up to 3 word, non-specific, idea] where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of jazz while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select from [INSERT field of human work or history] but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get [INSERT compelling term] with this one.",
      "style": "Modern jazz, close-miked expressive lead vocal with conversational phrasing, tasteful vocalese between lines, elastic swing, brushed drums and upright bass supporting warm piano voicings with subtle horn replies, restrained opening rising to a cathartic final chorus, organic room ambience and clear live-band mix\n\nTake 2: Jazz noir, elastic swing with late-night club phrasing; organic room ambience; intimate piano trio with brushed drums and upright bass; warm close-miked lead vocal with syncopated entrances, conversational verses, memorable sing-along refrain, and tasteful scat-like vowel turns in a sparse dramatic arrangement.",
      "lyric": "— TAKE 1 —\n\n[Verse 1]\nYou came in carrying rain in your coat,\nA little blue ticket, a barely held note,\nYou said, “I’m all right,” with your hand on the door,\nBut your eyes kept asking what silence was for.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nSo I poured out the evening, slow and low,\nLet the old hard story find somewhere to go.\n\n[Chorus]\nStay, stay—let the true thing be true,\nI’ve got a small brave room here for you.\nSay, say—what the daylight won’t say,\nWe can loosen the knot without throwing away.\nOh, ah—let it roll, let it rise,\nThere’s a whole new morning behind those eyes.\nStay, stay—if the world says you’re through,\nI’ll keep one honest chair here for you.\n\n[Verse 2]\nLike a night-shift nurse at the edge of the dawn,\nYou learned how to smile while the strength carried on.\nBut mercy came late with its sleeves rolled up,\nSet a warm little truth in your half-empty cup.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nAnd the ache had a name, though it trembled to speak,\nNot a weakness to hide, just a life at its peak.\n\n[Chorus]\nStay, stay—let the true thing be true,\nI’ve got a small brave room here for you.\nSay, say—what the daylight won’t say,\nWe can loosen the knot without throwing away.\nOh, ah—let it roll, let it rise,\nThere’s a whole new morning behind those eyes.\nStay, stay—if the world says you’re through,\nI’ll keep one honest chair here for you.\n\n[Bridge]\n(doo-ba, doo-ba, ah—carry it home)\nNo saint, no savior, no polished parade,\nJust two tired hearts and the promise we made:\nIf the floor gives way, we will name what we feel,\nIf the scar starts to sing, we will let it be real.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nStay, stay—now the true thing is true,\nThere’s a wider bright room opening through you.\nSay, say—let the old fear decay,\nWe are more than the worst thing that happened one day.\nOh, ah—let it roll, let it rise,\nHear the whole wide morning in your eyes.\nStay, stay—when the long night is through,\nYou won’t need that chair, but I’ll save it for you.\n\n[Outro]\n(ah-ooh, ah-ooh)\nLet it be true.\nLet it be you.\nLet it be new.\n\n— TAKE 2 —\n\n[Verse 1]\nYou left your coffee by the ledger,\nHalf a moon inside the cup;\nI read the figures, read the silence,\nCouldn’t make the numbers up.\nThe foreman called, the clock kept leaning,\nToward a dawn we couldn’t own;\nI wore your name beneath my collar,\nLike a key without a home.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nOh, easy now, don’t hurry the sorrow,\nLet it loosen, let it lie;\nWhat we carry into tomorrow\nDoesn’t have to testify.\n\n[Chorus]\nAh, let it go—go slow, go sweet,\nTurn that ache into a street;\nIf the heart breaks, let it break wide,\nLeave a little room inside.\nAh, say my name—no shame, no plea,\nWhat was lost still lives in me;\nI can bend, but I won’t bow,\nI am becoming someone now.\n\n[Verse 2]\nI found your glove behind the boiler,\nDust like winter on the seam;\nFunny how a thing so little\nCan outweigh a whole machine.\nWe were young enough for forever,\nOld enough to count the cost;\nEvery promise punched a timecard,\nEvery hour marked the loss.\n\n[Bridge]\n(Ooh—ah, carry me)\nNot to where the old doors opened,\nNot to where the sirens ran;\nTake me to the room I’m making,\nTake me as I am.\n\n[Chorus]\nAh, let it go—go slow, go sweet,\nTurn that ache into a street;\nIf the heart breaks, let it break wide,\nLeave a little room inside.\nAh, say my name—no shame, no plea,\nWhat was lost still lives in me;\nI can bend, but I won’t bow,\nI am becoming someone now.\n\n[Outro]\nGo slow, go sweet—\nI am becoming someone now.\n(Ooh—ah, somehow)",
