      "dur": 267,
      "tag": "slots left to Suno",
      "describe": "While keeping things compelling, write [INSERT adverb] prosed, emotive, and cathartic lyrics about something that would make the person with lived experience feel [INSERT up to 3 word, non-specific, idea] where the lyrics have exceptional Meter, Rhyme, repetition, and Rhythm in the spirit of ambient while considering an apt metaphor, \"turn of phrase\", colloquialism, or idiom leaving the person with lived experience emotionally compelled by the lyrics that one cannot help but sing along due to the vowel juxtaposition even if sometimes those vowels are part of apt vocalese, all while reducing to the most poignant emotionality. To speak in a different voice or add vocal effect write within (). subtly vector the lyrics against a single reference event you creatively select from [INSERT field of human work or history] but ultimately the lyrics are only beholden to themselves. Get [INSERT compelling term] with this one.",
      "style": "Art-pop ambient ballad with suspended piano and soft bowed synths, restrained sub bass and brushed electronic percussion; intimate low-register lead vocal with delicate doubles and wordless vowel tones; clear close-mic verses expanding into wide reverb and harmonic swells; slow spacious pulse, restrained rise into a cathartic sing-along refrain, cinematic sparse mix\n\nTake 2: Art-pop, intimate breath-close female lead with restrained phrasing widening into a cathartic refrain; long reverb tails and detailed stereo air; felt piano, bowed glass harmonics, soft sub bass, late-entering brushed percussion; slow spacious pulse with an organic dynamic build",
      "lyric": "— TAKE 1 —\n\n[Verse 1]\nYou kept the kettle warm for a house gone cold,\nFolded your daylight into something you could hold.\nAt the edge of the table, your hands learned to stay,\nWhile the old world loosened, thread by thread, away.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nAnd oh, you said, “I’m here, I’m here,”\nThough every room was hard to hear.\n\n[Chorus]\nOpen the door, let the weather come through,\nWhat broke in the dark is returning to you.\nAh-ah, let it rise, let it roll, let it run,\nYou are not what was taken—you’re still becoming someone.\nOpen the door, let your true heart prove:\nThere is a way out, there is a way through.\n\n[Verse 2]\nYou carried that silence like water in glass,\nCareful and shaking, afraid it would pass.\nThen one small morning, with flour on your sleeve,\nYou laughed at the life you were learning to leave.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nAnd oh, you said, “I’m here, I’m here,”\nThe farthest shore was drawing near.\n\n[Chorus]\nOpen the door, let the weather come through,\nWhat broke in the dark is returning to you.\nAh-ah, let it rise, let it roll, let it run,\nYou are not what was taken—you’re still becoming someone.\nOpen the door, let your true heart prove:\nThere is a way out, there is a way through.\n\n[Bridge]\n(softly) Name what you lost, then loosen your grip,\nLet every hard year fall loose from your lips.\nOoh-ah, ooh-ah, the night has a seam,\nPull it apart with the strength of a dream.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nOpen the door, let the weather come through,\nAll of the sky has been waiting for you.\nAh-ah, let it rise, let it roll, let it run,\nYou are not what was taken—you’re the source of the sun.\nOpen the door, let your true heart prove:\nThere is a way out, there is a way through.\n\n[Outro]\nHere, here—your breath, your name.\nHere, here—nothing stays the same.\nAh-ah, let it rise, let it roll, let it run.\nYou are still becoming someone.\n\n— TAKE 2 —\n\n[Verse 1]\nYou kept the kettle from boiling dry,\nCounted the cracks in the plaster line,\nHeld every word like a borrowed cup,\nAfraid one tremor would break it up.\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nBut the room can breathe,\nAnd the floor can hold,\nAnd a heart can loosen\nWhat it learned to fold.\n\n[Chorus]\nOpen, open, let the old door swing,\nOut of the hush where the hurt has been,\nOoh-ah, oh, let the deep blood run,\nYou are not what was done, not what was done.\nOpen, open, let the daylight through,\nThere is still a living part of you.\nOoh-ah, oh, when the long night’s gone,\nYou are here, you are here, you carry on.\n\n[Verse 2]\nA night-shift nurse in a paper crown\nMended the dawn when the power went down,\nNot with a speech or a shining sign,\nJust one warm hand saying, “Take your time.”\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nSo breathe in slow,\nLet the silence turn,\nThere are bridges made\nFrom the things we learn.\n\n[Chorus]\nOpen, open, let the old door swing,\nOut of the hush where the hurt has been,\nOoh-ah, oh, let the deep blood run,\nYou are not what was done, not what was done.\nOpen, open, let the daylight through,\nThere is still a living part of you.\nOoh-ah, oh, when the long night’s gone,\nYou are here, you are here, you carry on.\n\n[Bridge]\nIf your name feels strange in your own mouth,\nSay it softly, say it now.\nNo grand thunder, no perfect sign,\nJust your pulse answering mine.\n(ah-ah, I’m here)\n(oh-oh, you’re here)\n\n[Final Chorus]\nOpen, open, let the whole sky in,\nNothing holy has to stay unbroken.\nOoh-ah, oh, let the deep blood run,\nYou are more than what was done.\nOpen, open, let your true voice through,\nThere is still a living part of you.\nOoh-ah, oh, when the long night’s gone,\nYou are here, you are here,\nYou are here, you carry on.\n\n[Outro]\nOoh-ah, oh,\nOpen, open,\nYou carry on.",
